TI  - Lygodium microphyllum, a new target for biological control
T2  - BIOLOGICAL CONTROL IN THE TROPICS
CP  - Symposium on Biological Control in the Tropics
AB  - Climbing maidenhair (also called old world climbing fern in the USA), Lygodium microphyllum (Cav.) R.Br. (Lygodiaceae), is a serious environmental weed in parts of Martin and Palm Beach Counties, Florida, and is rapidly invading nearby areas. Growing in tropical and subtropical wetlands and areas with moist soil. L. microphyllum is well adapted to much of Florida, where its current rate of spread and environmental, impact is extremely serious and alarming. (Another species of climbing fern, L. japonicum, is also invasive and widely distributed in the south-eastern US.)
   The native range of L. microphyllum is tropical Africa, south-east Asia, north and eastern Australia and Melanesia where it grows in patches causing few if any problems. In Florida, L. microphyllum grows as a thick blanket over the ground, smothering native vegetation climbing to the canopy of tall trees. It is threatening the biodiversity of areas and there are concerns for the safety of some already-endangered plant species. Several environmentally sensitive areas in Florida are in danger of being completely overrun, including Everglades tree islands, pinelands, cypress swamps and open wetland marshes.
   L. microphyllum seriously alters fire ecology and the use of fire as a management tool. Fires spread rapidly over the ground mats and the fern carries the fire to the forest canopy by acting as a 'flame ladder'. During fires, burning mats of the fern dislodge from tree canopies to be carried by heat plumes a considerable distance to start new outbreaks.
   Following its introduction to Florida as an ornamental plant probably in the early 1960s, the population of L. microphyllum increased greatly until by 1997 nearly 20,000 hectares were infested. Herbicidal and physical controls have had variable results and may themselves threaten non-target plant species. Biological control not only offers the potential to reduce infestation sizes and limit spread of the weed, but also offers environmental safety.
   Scientists from the United States Department of Agriculture, Agricultural Research Service (USDA/ARS) have commenced an exploratory program to locate, identify and undertake host-specificity testing on potential biological control agents of the weed. Biological control has been successfully used to control another fern, Salvinia molesta. We hope and expect to be able to locate damaging and host-specific agents that will control L. microphyllum without affecting the many valued native ferns in Florida.
   Initial exploration for natural enemies in Australia and south-east Asia has commenced at the USDA Australian Biological Control Laboratory in Brisbane. Herbivores representing Coleoptera, Hemiptera, Hymenoptera, Lepidoptera and Thysanoptera have been collected, including a very damaging pyralid moth, Cataclysta camptozonale (Hampson), identified from Australia. During 1999, exploration will be expanded in this region and further study on the biology and host-specificity of herbivores already collected will be undertaken. An exploration program covering L. microphyllum in tropical Africa will be based at the USDA's Fort Lauderdale laboratories in Florida.
