AU  - Banks, PB
AU  - Possell, M
AU  - Price, CJ
TI  - Visit, investigate, ignore: Olfactory misinformation reduces browsing damage to valued seedlings by a mammalian herbivore
T2  - BIOLOGICAL CONSERVATION
AB  - Foraging by mammalian herbivores critically impacts threatened plants and ecosystems globally, resulting in numerous conservation challenges. Like many animals, herbivores incorporate olfactory cures to forage efficiently. When 'misinformation', i.e. misleading odour cues, degrade the reliability of odour information, forage efficiency often declines. How browsing mammalian herbivores respond to olfactory misinformation is unknown, but of interest, given their impacts. Using an Australian plant-herbivore system, we tested whether olfactory misinformation altered the behaviour of wild herbivores foraging for a preferred plant. Swamp wallabies (Wallabia bicolor) are mixed feeders, filling similar ecological roles to browsing herbivores such as Odocoileus deer in the Americas, and browse Eucalyptus seedlings, the foundation genus of many forests and woodlands. We deployed unrewarding odour from leaves surrounding focal seedlings, reducing the reliability with which the odour would predict the plant's location. Despite wallabies visiting seedlings surrounded by unrewarding odour at a similar rate to control seedlings, seedlings surrounded by unrewarding odour were 80 % less likely than controls to be browsed over two weeks. These results suggest that misinformation interferes with the ability of herbivores to recognise target plants as desirable food sources. Olfactory misinformation has potential to be used as a practical, non-lethal management strategy which, through harnessing the natural foraging behaviours of mammalian herbivores, could act to decrease their browsing pressure and mitigate their impact on vulnerable plants and ecosystems.
