ED  - Luoma, DL
ED  - Meinke, RJ
ED  - Wilson, MV
TI  - Ecology and management of the endangered western lily (Lilium occidentale) in northwestern California
T2  - CONSERVATION AND MANAGEMENT OF NATIVE PLANTS AND FUNGI
CP  - Oregon Conference on the Conservation and Management of Native Vascular Plants, Bryophytes, and Fungi
AB  - The endangered western lily is restricted to the immediate coast of northern California and southern Oregon. Approximately 32 sites are known between Eureka, California and Coos Bay, Oregon. Although the habitat for the lily appears diverse (coastal scrub/prairie, freshwater marsh, spruce forest), most sites share similarities in soils and associated species. Principal historical impacts were from farming and other human developments, and encroachment by vegetation. Most habitat appears subject to rapid modification by vegetative succession. Fire was undoubtedly important prior to white settlement, and since then cattle grazing has been instrumental in maintaining habitat in suitable condition for the lily. A monitoring program was begun in the mid-1980s at the four sites at Table Bluff, California, and was recently expanded to about 16 sites rangewide. Management actions at various sites have included, in addition to monitoring, exclusion of cattle, deer, and small mammals; seasonal introduction of cattle; removal of spruce and scrub, and mowing of grassland; establishment of a new colony; and greenhouse propagation of a seedling bank for future reintroduction projects. Flowering by the lily increased more than 2000 percent after eight years of management at one site. The monitoring program, methods, and results of management as well as recommendations for future management are described in detail.
