TI  - EFFECTS OF FERAL GOATS (CAPRA-HIRCUS) ON ISLAND ECOSYSTEMS
T2  - BIOLOGICAL CONSERVATION
AB  - Feral goats (C. hircus) are implicated in habitat destruction and alteration of species composition on sensitive insular ecosystems. In the absence of population control, goats have become the ecologically dominant species on many islands with the result that numerous endemic plant species have been extirpated, or are threatened by excessive grazing. Removal of goats can lead to rapid recovery of suppressed vegetation. The problems associated with excessive numbers of feral goats are rarely studied or formally recognized. Extensive and intensive research is critically needed if affected island ecosystems are to be preserved or restored.
