AU  - Li, RF
AU  - Kong, Q
AU  - Liu, Q
AU  - Niu, HS
TI  - Grazing exclusion does not affect the water use efficiency of alpine steppe plants in central Tibet
T2  - GLOBAL ECOLOGY AND CONSERVATION
AB  - Grazing exclusion is a key strategy for rehabilitating degraded grasslands, but its effect on plant water use efficiency (WUE) is not well understood. Given that carbon isotope discrimination (S13C) is a well-established proxy for WUE, this study investigated interspecific variations in S13C across five dominant alpine steppe plant species (Astragalus arnoldii, Stipa purpurea, Leontopodium nanum, Kobresia pygmaea, Carex moorcroftii) in response to varying durations of grazing exclusion (6, 11, and 12 years) in central Tibet. Results showed that S13C remained stable over time across species, with significant interspecific differences. S. purpurea and A. arnoldii had higher S13C values than L. nanum, while K. pygmaea and C. moorcroftii had intermediate values. Redundancy analysis (RDA) indicated that WUE in S. purpurea, C. moorcroftii, and A. arnoldii positively correlated with soil moisture content, whereas L. nanum and K. pygmaea showed negative correlations. These findings suggest that WUE is largely determined by species taxonomy rather than grazing exclusion. Divergent water use strategies may facilitate species coexistence through niche differentiation. Preserving taxonomic diversity through grazing exclusion can enhance long-term restoration success by maintaining functional redundancy and adaptive potential in alpine steppe ecosystems. Considering plant WUE alongside other ecological factors and management practices is essential for optimizing grassland restoration outcomes.
