TI  - Grazing intensity affects the composition and spatial heterogeneity of plant functional groups in a desert steppe
T2  - GLOBAL ECOLOGY AND CONSERVATION
AB  - The spatial heterogeneity of plants within a community reflects the distribution of species and their responses to grazing disturbance. Understanding these processes is essential for clarifying the dynamics and stability of plant community structure. However, while existing studies have focused on the effects of grazing on spatial heterogeneity at the community or population level, the impact of grazing intensity on the spatial heterogeneity of plant functional groups remains unclear. In this study, we investigated the spatial distribution patterns of perennial grasses, shrubs and semi-shrubs, perennial forbs, and annual and biennial plants under four grazing intensities (control/no grazing, light grazing, moderate grazing, and heavy grazing) in a Stipa breviflora desert steppe in Inner Mongolia, China. The results show that perennial grasses displayed weak spatial heterogeneity under the control and light grazing, influenced by non-treatment factors. Compared to the control, grazing treatments simplified the distribution of shrubs and semishrubs, with weak spatial heterogeneity. Perennial forbs and annual and biennial plants exhibited patchy and aggregated distributions under light and moderate grazing, showing strong spatial heterogeneity influenced by grazing disturbances. With increasing grazing intensity, the species associations among communities weaken. Under non-grazing conditions, dominant species are widely distributed across all four plant functional groups, whereas under heavy grazing, the dominant species shift to perennial grasses. Our study highlights that light grazing, through grazing disturbances such as grazing and trampling, led to the fragmentation of perennial grasses and a reduction in the abundance of shrubs and semi-shrubs, thereby creating more growing space for perennial forbs and increasing their spatial heterogeneity and dominance. In contrast, heavy grazing enhanced the aggregation of grazing-tolerant perennial grasses, allowing them to become the dominant group within the plant community. These findings provide theoretical support for sustainable grassland grazing management and restoration strategies.
